From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests: tolerate a riscv64 JVM stdout warning in
 test_jvm_options

test_jvm_options spawns a subprocess that imports jnius (starting the JVM)
and then json.dumps the JVM's own input arguments straight to stdout,
which the parent process parses with json.loads(stdoutdata.decode()).

On riscv64 the JVM itself prints a startup diagnostic to stdout before
any Python code runs:

    WARNING: Not able to assign machine() = riscv64 to a cpu value!
             Using cpu = 'riscv64' instead!

That line lands ahead of the JSON in the same stdout stream, so
json.loads sees "WARNING: ...\n[...]" and fails with
JSONDecodeError: Expecting value: line 1 column 1 (char 0), even though
the JVM started fine and the arguments round-tripped correctly. The
child's own json.dump() output is always the last line written, so
parse only that line instead of the whole stream.

Upstream-Status: To upstream [not yet submitted; riscv64-specific JVM startup diagnostic, no reference to check it against yet]

Signed-off-by: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
---
--- a/tests/test_jvm_options.py
+++ b/tests/test_jvm_options.py
@@ -27,5 +27,8 @@
             bufsize=-1, stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE)
         stdoutdata, _ = process.communicate(json.dumps(options).encode())
         assert process.wait() == 0
-        actual_options = json.loads(stdoutdata.decode())
+        # The JVM itself can print a startup diagnostic to stdout ahead of
+        # the child's own json.dump() output; that dump is always the last
+        # line written, so parse only it.
+        actual_options = json.loads(stdoutdata.decode().splitlines()[-1])
         assert list(sorted(options)) == list(sorted(actual_options))
